e dot product to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8k+16 & =0 \\\\ 8k & =16 \\\\ k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-39324" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ce03934dd8681099f3b791de03dd62be1d5945c02fb1f7e22fbb089bb563b8e"} {"question" "q-39324" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-39324" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "14"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-39324" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74092" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal vectors, then $k=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74092" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot k+(-3)\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-4)+(-5)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2k-9-4+5 \\\\  & =2k-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the dot product to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k-8 & =0 \\\\ 2k & =8 \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74092" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ce03934dd8681099f3b791de03dd62be1d5945c02fb1f7e22fbb089bb563b8e"} {"question" "q-74092" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74092" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74092" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $a⊥b.$ Find the value of $k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $a$ and $b$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1}+{a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{2}+{a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{3}+{a}_{4}\\cdot {b}_{4} \\\\  & =k\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-3)+k\\cdot k \\\\  & =2k+3-3+{k}^{2} \\\\  & =2k+{k}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the dot product to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+{k}^{2} & =0 \\\\ k(2+k) & =0 \\\\ k & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ce03934dd8681099f3b791de03dd62be1d5945c02fb1f7e22fbb089bb563b8e"} {"question" "q-74093" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74093" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "k=-2,0"] ["math" "k=0"] ["math" "k=0,2"] ["math" "k=2"] ["text" "No solution"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74093" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "k=-2,0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-140123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}3k \\\\ -6 \\\\ k \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that  $u⊥v.$ Find the value of $k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-140123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =k\\cdot 3k+1\\cdot (-6)+3\\cdot k+(-2)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3{k}^{2}-6+3k+0 \\\\  & =3({k}^{2}+k-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the dot product to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({k}^{2}+k-2) & =0 \\\\ 3(k+2)(k-1) & =0 \\\\ k & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-140123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ce03934dd8681099f3b791de03dd62be1d5945c02fb1f7e22fbb089bb563b8e"} {"question" "q-140123" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-140123" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "k=-1,1"] ["math" "k=-1,2"] ["math" "k=-2,1"] ["math" "k=-2,2"] ["math" "k=1,2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-140123" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "k=-2,1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-39333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u\\,⊥\\,v.$ Find $(v+w)\\cdot u+v\\cdot (u+w).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-39333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot v=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(v+w)\\cdot u+v\\cdot (u+w) & =v\\cdot u+w\\cdot u+v\\cdot u+v\\cdot w \\\\  & =0+w\\cdot u+0+v\\cdot w \\\\  & =w\\cdot u+v\\cdot w \\\\  & =((-3)\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & +(2\\cdot (-3)+4\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 5+(-1)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =(-9-4+5+4)+(-6+8+0+2) \\\\  & =-4+4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-39333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ce03934dd8681099f3b791de03dd62be1d5945c02fb1f7e22fbb089bb563b8e"} {"question" "q-39333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-39333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-14"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-39333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74181" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal. Find $(2u+3w)\\cdot v+u\\cdot (v-w).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74181" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot v=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2u+3w)\\cdot v+u\\cdot (v-w) & =(2u)\\cdot v+(3w)\\cdot v+u\\cdot v-u\\cdot w \\\\  & =2(u\\cdot v)+3(w\\cdot v)+u\\cdot v-u\\cdot w \\\\  & =0+3(w\\cdot v)+0-u\\cdot w \\\\  & =3(w\\cdot v)-u\\cdot w \\\\  & =3(0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & -(0\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =3(0+2+0+-2)-(0-1-9-4) \\\\  & =0-(-14) \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74181" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ce03934dd8681099f3b791de03dd62be1d5945c02fb1f7e22fbb089bb563b8e"} {"question" "q-74181" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74181" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-11"] ["math" "-14"] ["math" "14"] ["math" "17"] ["math" "21"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74181" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "14"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74180" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ and $w$ are orthogonal. Find $(u-2v)\\cdot w+u\\cdot (v+2w).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74180" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $u$ and $w$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot w=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u-2v)\\cdot w+u\\cdot (v+2w) & =u\\cdot w-(2v)\\cdot w+u\\cdot v+u\\cdot (2w) \\\\  & =u\\cdot w-2(v\\cdot w)+u\\cdot v+2(u\\cdot w) \\\\  & =0-2(v\\cdot w)+u\\cdot v+0 \\\\  & =-2(v\\cdot w)+u\\cdot v \\\\  & =-2(2\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & +(2\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =-2(-4+0+0-1)+(4+0-6-5) \\\\  & =10-7 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74180" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ce03934dd8681099f3b791de03dd62be1d5945c02fb1f7e22fbb089bb563b8e"} {"question" "q-74180" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74180" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12"] ["math" "-17"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74180" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-140125" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u\\,⊥\\,w.$ Find $(w+v)\\cdot (2u+3w).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-140125" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $u$ and $w$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot w=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(w+v)\\cdot (2u+3w) & =2(w\\cdot u)+2(v\\cdot u)+3(w\\cdot w)+3(v\\cdot w) \\\\  & =0+2(v\\cdot u)+3(w\\cdot w)+3(v\\cdot w) \\\\  & =2(v\\cdot u)+3(w\\cdot w)+3(v\\cdot w) \\\\  & =2(-4\\cdot 5+(-2)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-3)+(-4)\\cdot 3) \\\\  & +3(1\\cdot 1+(-5)\\cdot (-5)+2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & +3(-4\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot (-5)+2\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2(-40)+3(34)+3(2) \\\\  & =-80+102+6 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-140125" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ce03934dd8681099f3b791de03dd62be1d5945c02fb1f7e22fbb089bb563b8e"} {"question" "q-140125" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-140125" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12"] ["math" "-58"] ["math" "-62"] ["math" "13"] ["math" "28"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-140125" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "28"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-39292" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u\\,⊥\\,w.$ Find $(u+v)\\cdot w+u\\cdot (v+w).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-39292" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $u$ and $w$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot w=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u+v)\\cdot w+u\\cdot (v+w) & =u\\cdot w+v\\cdot w+u\\cdot v+u\\cdot w \\\\  & =0+v\\cdot w+u\\cdot v+0 \\\\  & =v\\cdot w+u\\cdot v \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 4+5\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 3) \\\\  & +(1\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 5+4\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =(6-4-5-6)+(3+3+10-8) \\\\  & =-9+8 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-39292" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ce03934dd8681099f3b791de03dd62be1d5945c02fb1f7e22fbb089bb563b8e"} {"question" "q-39292" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-39292" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-28"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "15"] ["math" "45"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-39292" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74195" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0 \\\\ b \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w.$ What is the value of $a-b?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74195" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 3+b\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ a+0+b-2 & =0 \\\\ a+b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 1+b\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 5 & =0 \\\\ -a+0+2b+5 & =0 \\\\ -a+2b & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}a+b=2 \\\\ -a+2b=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the first equation to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n3b=-3\\,⟹\\,b=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b=-1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+(-1) & =2 \\\\ a & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na-b=3-(-1)=4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74195" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ce03934dd8681099f3b791de03dd62be1d5945c02fb1f7e22fbb089bb563b8e"} {"question" "q-74195" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74195" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74195" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ a \\\\ b \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u⊥v$ and $u⊥w.$ What is the value of $a-b?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given that $u⊥v$ and $u⊥w$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot (-2)+a\\cdot 1+b\\cdot 5+5\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ -4+a+5b-10 & =0 \\\\ a+5b & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot 1+a\\cdot 4+b\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ 2+4a-b+5 & =0 \\\\ 4a-b & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}a+5b=14 \\\\ 4a-b=-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-4$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-21b=-63\\,⟹\\,b=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b=3$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+5(3) & =14 \\\\ a+15 & =14 \\\\ a & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na-b=-1-3=-4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ce03934dd8681099f3b791de03dd62be1d5945c02fb1f7e22fbb089bb563b8e"} {"question" "q-74192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-74192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-40026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6 \\\\ b+1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u⊥v$ and $u⊥w.$ What is the value of $a+b?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-40026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given that $u⊥v$ and $u⊥w$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ 1\\cdot b+a\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ b-3a-6-8 & =0 \\\\ b-3a & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 5+a\\cdot 6+3\\cdot (b+1)+2\\cdot 5 & =0 \\\\ 5+6a+3b+3+10 & =0 \\\\ 6a+3b & =-18 \\\\ 2a+b & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3a+b=14 \\\\ 2a+b=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-1$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n5a=-20\\,⟹\\,a=-4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=-4$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3(-4)+b & =14 \\\\ 12+b & =14 \\\\ b & =14-12 \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na+b=-4+2=-2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-40026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ce03934dd8681099f3b791de03dd62be1d5945c02fb1f7e22fbb089bb563b8e"} {"question" "q-40026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-40026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-40026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-39337" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 3 \\\\ b \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w.$ What is the value of $a+b?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-39337" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-5)+b\\cdot 3+(-5)\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ 2a-15+3b+20 & =0 \\\\ 2a+3b & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot (-4)+3\\cdot (-2)+b\\cdot 5+(-5)\\cdot 3 & =0 \\\\ -4a-6+5b-15 & =0 \\\\ -4a+5b & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2a+3b=-5 \\\\ -4a+5b=21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n11b=11\\,⟹\\,b=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b=1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a+3(1) & =-5 \\\\ 2a+3 & =-5 \\\\ 2a & =-8 \\\\ a & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na+b=-4+1=-3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-39337" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ce03934dd8681099f3b791de03dd62be1d5945c02fb1f7e22fbb089bb563b8e"} {"question" "q-39337" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-39337" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "-5"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-39337" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-40024" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ a \\\\ b \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u⊥v$ and $u⊥w.$ What is the value of $a+b?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-40024" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given that $u⊥v$ and $u⊥w$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ -3\\cdot 3+a\\cdot 4+b\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ -9+4a+b-4 & =0 \\\\ 4a+b & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ -3\\cdot 1+a\\cdot 1+b\\cdot (-3)-4\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ -3+a-3b+16 & =0 \\\\ a-3b & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4a+b=13 \\\\ a-3b=-13\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $3$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13a=26\\,⟹\\,a=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=2$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2)+b & =13 \\\\ 8+b & =13 \\\\ b & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na+b=2+5=7.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-40024" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ce03934dd8681099f3b791de03dd62be1d5945c02fb1f7e22fbb089bb563b8e"} {"question" "q-40024" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-40024" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-\\frac{47}{13}"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "\\frac{95}{13}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "q-40024" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/state.json" "file_sha256" "56f304f8890eaa4f2636f91cbc3f677f46d197552a57c2617b406e4f0a9e1b4c"} {"question" "e-5909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/b8e1e42d3c85f7b3783a0c46d5075e0c588f22a030f3d6a84da9ad3444ad35d0.png), which of the following vectors are orthogonal to $\\mathbf{u}$?\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/6e69c8002a518487ce4821efe1faae85ba7ec365b1bedf23d7bfcd30e10e42b5.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/example-5909.json" "file_sha256" "429ef0495d0a128ff044dfdc9dcc4880a3c708ac6698854405f8ec9edfcf578d"} {"question" "e-5909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We compute the dot product between $\\mathbf{u}$ and each of the listed vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-4)+0\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =-5+9-8+0 \\\\  & =-4.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {w}_{4} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 8+0\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-10-6+16+0 \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot x & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {x}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {x}_{4} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot (-8)+0\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-10+12-16+0 \\\\  & =-14.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $\\mathbf{w}$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/example-5909.json" "file_sha256" "429ef0495d0a128ff044dfdc9dcc4880a3c708ac6698854405f8ec9edfcf578d"} {"question" "e-5551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/16e82b08842bdec1de657e14110b08037cca4452d1f0e8c64a8fd783037adeb6.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/559785dc3071ba5018f9b97d45e18e9f7bf937f702f9eeaf0a9c5ae087d5b43f.png) are orthogonal vectors, find the value of $k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/example-5551.json" "file_sha256" "f0838114d603410386db635878f99ce6533dde40f9337630df8ef34f106d90d2"} {"question" "e-5551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-4)+(-3)\\cdot 6+k\\cdot (-6)+(-2)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =-20-18-6k-16 \\\\  & =-6k-54.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the last line to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6k-54 & =0 \\\\ -6k & =54 \\\\ k & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/example-5551.json" "file_sha256" "f0838114d603410386db635878f99ce6533dde40f9337630df8ef34f106d90d2"} {"question" "e-5552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/61cb89adc9a0cce6a434c41be5d2d1799e01e94bebe1b4f44d48bc011c1a349b.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/00af13e5017635a0b333ecd5fc2bfc761c9612427b8a3986ba48fa904ff2eed0.png) be vectors such that $\\mathbf{u} \\!\\perp\\! \\mathbf{w}$. Find $(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})\\cdot \\mathbf{w} + \\mathbf{u}\\cdot(\\mathbf{v}+ \\mathbf{w}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/example-5552.json" "file_sha256" "bd736738a4a59e19f5b969de99cef3d35b93ed23a5ce24394cd0ec4005db42bf"} {"question" "e-5552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}=0$. Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u+v)\\cdot w+u\\cdot (v+w) & =u\\cdot w+v\\cdot w+u\\cdot v+u\\cdot w \\\\  & =0+v\\cdot w+u\\cdot v+0 \\\\  & =v\\cdot w+u\\cdot v \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 1) \\\\  & +((-1)\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-3)+5\\cdot 4)\\, \\\\  & =(-2-9+4)+(-2+6+20) \\\\  & =-7+24 \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/example-5552.json" "file_sha256" "bd736738a4a59e19f5b969de99cef3d35b93ed23a5ce24394cd0ec4005db42bf"} {"question" "e-5553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/618da6573504088a90064c503b3603f9c1cbedaee493473db435765ca635bdb9.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/a71f47b53dab435e794fa5c4c889141b8ce32c93715abd56e0dc48d6535865b8.png) be vectors such that $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$. What is the value of $a+b?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/example-5553.json" "file_sha256" "0cc8a66983e6fbab6b7c6a590abea2a6c3ae87d17311ead22b16ba58b4a940c7"} {"question" "e-5553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given that $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v}=0$. Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ -8\\cdot 6+a\\cdot (-15)+b\\cdot 9+(-10)\\cdot (-12) & =0 \\\\ -48-15a+9b+120 & =0 \\\\ -15a+9b & =-72 \\\\ -5a+3b & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ -8\\cdot 4+a\\cdot 2+b\\cdot (-5)+(-10)\\cdot (-3) & =0 \\\\ -32+2a-5b+30 & =0 \\\\ 2a-5b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-5a+3b=-24 \\\\ 2a-5b=2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $5$, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-10a+6b=-48 \\\\ 10a-25b=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, adding the above equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n-19b = -38 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad b = 2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b = 2$ into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a-5(2) & =2 \\\\ 2a-10 & =2 \\\\ 2a & =12 \\\\ a & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na+b = 6 + 2 = 8.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/example-5553.json" "file_sha256" "0cc8a66983e6fbab6b7c6a590abea2a6c3ae87d17311ead22b16ba58b4a940c7"}] "usage" {"q-39337" [] "e-5551" [] "q-39333" [] "e-5909" [] "q-40024" [] "e-5553" [] "e-5552" []} "basis" 1658 "retractions" [[17592186134134 :question/problem "Let $u = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, v = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ be vectors such that $u⊥v$. Find $(v + w) \\cdot u + v \\cdot (u + w)$."] [17592186134136 :question/problem "Let $u = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 3 \\\\ b \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}, v = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$, and $w = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ be vectors such that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w$. What is the value of $a + b$?"] [17592186134177 :question/problem "Let $u = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ a \\\\ b \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}, v = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$, and $w = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ be vectors such that $u⊥v$ and $u⊥w$. What is the value of $a + b$?"] [17592186133553 :question/problem "Given the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$, which of the following vectors are orthogonal to $\\mathbf{u}$?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186133553 :question/worked-solution "We compute the dot product between $\\mathbf{u}$ and each of the listed vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu \\cdot v &= u_{1} \\cdot v_{1} + u_{2} \\cdot v_{2} + u_{3} \\cdot v_{3} + u_{4} \\cdot v_{4} \\\\\n&= (-5) \\cdot 1 + (-3) \\cdot (-3) + 2 \\cdot (-4) + 0 \\cdot 9 \\\\\n&=-5 + 9 - 8 + 0 \\\\\n&=-4.\\cdot\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu \\cdot w &= u_{1} \\cdot w_{1} + u_{2} \\cdot w_{2} + u_{3} \\cdot w_{3} + u_{4} \\cdot w_{4} \\\\\n&= (-5) \\cdot 2 + (-3) \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot 8 + 0 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&=-10 - 6 + 16 + 0 \\\\\n&= 0.✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu \\cdot x &= u_{1} \\cdot x_{1} + u_{2} \\cdot x_{2} + u_{3} \\cdot x_{3} + u_{4} \\cdot x_{4} \\\\\n&= (-5) \\cdot 2 + (-3) \\cdot (-4) + 2 \\cdot (-8) + 0 \\cdot 6 \\\\\n&=-10 + 12 - 16 + 0 \\\\\n&=-14.\\cdot\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $\\mathbf{w}$ only."] [17592186133500 :question/problem "If $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ k \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$ are orthogonal vectors, find the value of $k$."] [17592186133500 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu \\cdot v &= u_{1} \\cdot v_{1} + u_{2} \\cdot v_{2} + u_{3} \\cdot v_{3} + u_{4} \\cdot v_{4} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot (-4) + (-3) \\cdot 6 + k \\cdot (-6) + (-2) \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&=-20 - 18 - 6k - 16 \\\\\n&=-6k - 54\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the last line to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6k - 54 &= 0 \\\\\n- 6k &= 54 \\\\\nk &= -9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133501 :question/problem "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ be vectors such that $\\mathbf{u} \\!\\perp\\! \\mathbf{w}$. Find $(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})\\cdot \\mathbf{w} + \\mathbf{u}\\cdot(\\mathbf{v}+ \\mathbf{w})$."] [17592186133501 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}=0\n$$\n\n. Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(u + v) \\cdot w + u \\cdot (v + w) &= u \\cdot w + v \\cdot w + u \\cdot v + u \\cdot w \\\\\n&= 0 + v \\cdot w + u \\cdot v + 0 \\\\\n&= v \\cdot w + u \\cdot v \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot (-1) + (-3) \\cdot 3 + 4 \\cdot 1) \\\\\n&= + ((-1) \\cdot 2 + (-2) \\cdot (-3) + 5 \\cdot 4) \\\\\n&= (-2 - 9 + 4) + (-2 + 6 + 20) \\\\\n&=-7 + 24 \\\\\n&= 17\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133502 :question/problem "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ a \\\\ b \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ be vectors such that $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$. What is the value of $a+b$?"] [17592186133502 :question/worked-solution "Given that $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$, we have\n\n$$\n{u \\cdot v = 0; u \\cdot w = 0\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v}=0\n$$\n\n. Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu \\cdot v &= 0 \\\\\n- 8 \\cdot 6 + a \\cdot (-15) + b \\cdot 9 + (-10) \\cdot (-12) &= 0 \\\\\n- 48 - 15a + 9b + 120 &= 0 \\\\\n- 15a + 9b &= -72 \\\\\n- 5a + 3b &= -24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}=0\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu \\cdot w &= 0 \\\\\n- 8 \\cdot 4 + a \\cdot 2 + b \\cdot (-5) + (-10) \\cdot (-3) &= 0 \\\\\n- 32 + 2a - 5b + 30 &= 0 \\\\\n2a - 5b &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n{-5a + 3b =-24; 2a - 5b = 2\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $5$, we get\n\n$$\n{-10a + 6b =-48; 10a - 25b = 10\n$$\n\nNow, adding the above equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n-19b = -38 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad b = 2\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b = 2$ into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2a - 5(2) &= 2 \\\\\n2a - 10 &= 2 \\\\\n2a &= 12 \\\\\na &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na+b = 6 + 2 = 8\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186131247 17592186131248 17592186131249 17592186131250 17592186131251 17592186131259 17592186131260 17592186131261 17592186131262 17592186131263 17592186131505 17592186131506 17592186131507 17592186131508 17592186131509] "immutable_fields" [[17592186131246 :answer-field/correct] [17592186131246 :answer-field/id] [17592186131246 :answer-field/key] [17592186131246 :answer-field/type] [17592186131258 :answer-field/correct] [17592186131258 :answer-field/id] [17592186131258 :answer-field/key] [17592186131258 :answer-field/type] [17592186131504 :answer-field/correct] [17592186131504 :answer-field/id] [17592186131504 :answer-field/key] [17592186131504 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
